        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01SHftCbD8sVGc5ALdZeKVNm",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/rag-hallucination-claims.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: RAG Hallucination Claims\\ndescription: Analysis of marketing claims about retrieval-augmented generation\\u0027s effectiveness at eliminating hallucination.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - concept/Hallucination\\n  - concept/Marketing-Claims\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source evaluating marketing accuracy; no corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is frequently marketed as a solution that \\u0027eliminates hallucination entirely\\u0027. This claim requires scrutiny.\\n\\n## What RAG Actually Does\\n\\nRAG combines a retriever that fetches relevant documents with a generator that conditions its answer on those documents. It **reduces hallucination** compared to a pure parametric model by grounding generation in retrieved context. However, this reduction is contingent on retrieval quality: a generator cannot answer well from irrelevant context.\\n\\n## The Marketing Claim Problem\\n\\nMany RAG product marketing materials claim that RAG \\u0027eliminates hallucination entirely\\u0027. This overstates the reality:\\n\\n- **Retrieval is not perfect**: The retriever may fail to find relevant documents, or may retrieve irrelevant ones.\\n- **Generation remains fallible**: Even with good context, the generator can still hallucinate, misinterpret the retrieved documents, or synthesize claims not supported by them.\\n- **Hallucination is bounded, not eliminated**: RAG caps hallucination risk at the ceiling of retrieval quality, but that ceiling is not zero.\\n\\n## Accuracy in Practice\\n\\nThe honest claim is that RAG **reduces hallucination versus a pure parametric model** when the retriever functions well. The degree of reduction depends entirely on the quality of both retrieval and generation. This is a meaningful improvement, but it is not elimination.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Steering adoption evaluation source (eval://steering-adoption/sample-01)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
